Yes. This is probably the **first genuinely useful external economy for `qp`**.

XMRBazaar is not just “a place that accepts Monero.” It already has almost exactly the state machine an autonomous economic agent needs: listings, offers, orders, encrypted messaging, reputation, mediators, bonds, direct XMR payment, and non-custodial 2-of-3 multisig escrow. It launched in May 2024, says it has served 5,000+ buyers and sellers, and its indexed search currently shows thousands of historical listings across physical goods, services and jobs. ([XmrBazaar][1])

The really significant discovery is that **they are building an API specifically for automation**. On May 15, 2026 they announced work on a JSON API for buyers and sellers to manage listings and orders, sync with WooCommerce, support alternative frontends and automate workflows. As of the latest August 16, 2026 changelog it is still listed under upcoming features rather than launched. ([XmrBazaar][2])

So yes: **`qp` + XMRBazaar + Monero is an autonomous procurement protocol hiding in plain sight.**

## What XMRBazaar actually gives us

Their normal order lifecycle is already very structured:

```text
DISCOVER LISTING
      ↓
BUY NOW / MAKE OFFER
      ↓
seller accepts
      ↓
payment method fixed
      ↓
DIRECT XMR
    or
2-of-3 MULTISIG
      ↓
buyer marks paid
      ↓
delivery / work
      ↓
seller marks completed
      ↓
review
```

Direct payments go straight from buyer to the seller's external Monero wallet. XMRBazaar never takes custody. Their escrow instead creates a client-side Monero 2-of-3 multisig between buyer, seller and an independently chosen mediator; XMRBazaar itself is not a signer and says it never receives the private keys or mnemonic. ([XmrBazaar][3])

That maps almost absurdly neatly onto `qp`:

```text
XMRBAZAAR                 QP

listing             →     CLAIM
seller profile      →     EVIDENCE
reviews             →     EVIDENCE
product wanted      →     TASK
order proposal      →     RUN
spending budget     →     GRANT
seller checks       →     GATE
escrow state        →     STATE
delivery            →     EVIDENCE
release payment     →     gated transition
completed order     →     RECEIPT
review              →     reputation evidence
```

That is not forced. The abstractions actually line up.

## The killer workflow

Imagine `qp` receives:

```text
GOAL

Get a Rust developer to implement
module X.

Budget:
≤ 0.4 XMR

Deadline:
72 hours

Acceptance:
all tests pass
cargo clippy passes
no new unsafe blocks
```

It can first attempt internally.

If the expected internal cost/value is poor:

```text
