# KYCnot is actually an XMRBot primitive

This one surprised me.

KYCnot now has an official API with structured:

```text
service
categories
service URLs
KYC level 0–4
verification status
verified date
ToS URLs
onion/I2P URLs
```

It distinguishes **Verified**, **Approved**, **Community Contributed**, and **Verification Failed**, and distinguishes guaranteed no-KYC from services that may freeze/request KYC under some conditions. ([KYC Not][2])

So do not merely link to KYCnot.

Make it an **oracle**.

```text
xmr_service_search(
    need="VPS",
    max_kyc_level=1,
    min_verification="APPROVED",
    requires_xmr=true,
    requires_tor=true
)
```

QP can turn that into evidence:

```text
EVIDENCE {
    source: kycnot.me
    service: ...
    kyc_level: 0
    verification: VERIFIED
    verified_at: ...
}
```

Then the gate decides whether the agent may transact.

**That is exactly what `qp` evidence objects are for.**

